            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has what could be considered average air leakage. However, there are some areas that you could see improvements. 



I recommend that you check around your baseboards and window frames for drafts. These can easily be sealed with caulking. 



You may also wish to do some air sealing in your crawlspace. Check the poly vapour barrier for tears, holes, and gaps at the seams, and seal these with caulking or tape. With the back of your hand, feel around the header space for drafts, and seal any that you find. 



Air leakage was also observed through your bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Other upgrades recommended in this report, such as heating upgrades, may also affect your air leakage rate. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $250 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.5 GJ.

</airSeal>
                <windows>

As almost all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass,  I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all your windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, In addition, Arctic Energy Alliance recommends triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Your large front picture window is already triple pane, but do not appear to have a low-E coating. I have included this window as well in my recommendations, but as it is in good physical condition I would not recommend an upgrade.



I noted that most of your windows are in good shape, but I did note a significant amount of condensation on both the front bedroom windows. I recommend that you keep the curtains on these windows open during the day to allow air to circulate and prevent condensation and frost build up, as these can lead to mould growth and air quality problems. 



Savings: 

By replacing the 16 windows in your house with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $620 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 18.2 GJ. There are no rebates available for window upgrades unless they are done with an exterior insulation retrofit. </windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $1,090 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 0.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Since I could not find any rating information on your current oil fired furnace, I modelled it using our default rating of 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $1,790 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 39.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program







</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS

While I did not focus on your crawlspace, I did notice while I was down there that there is some missing insulation on one of the walls, and that there was water in the crawlspace



I recommend that you replace any wet or missing pink fibreglass insulation in your crawlspace before winter, perhaps in conjunction with some air sealing.



I also recommend if you continue to have water in the basement, you add ventilation to the crawlspace walls to allow air to circulate through the basement during the summer and allow any water to dry. The vents can be closed and sealed in the winter to prevent heat loss. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DEPRESSURIZATION TEST

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, bathroom fans, clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 6.1 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and wood burning fireplace are both chimney vented, and could be susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into your house.



If you upgrade the furnace to a sealed combustion unit (as outlined elsewhere in this report), that type of furnace is not susceptible to back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke detectors, but I did not see a carbon monoxide (CO) detector. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED LIGHTS

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to florescent lighting. Floresecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



